Drop post-quantum algorithms this build can never negotiate.

Both ends of a quictun tunnel are this same binary, so what gets negotiated
is a property of this build rather than of an unknown peer -- and it is never
any of these:

  - ML-KEM and Kyber are absent from DefaultSupportedGroupIds() (X25519,
    P-256, P-384), so no handshake can select them as a key share. A peer
    cannot steer the TLS 1.3 server into one either: the group_id it passes to
    SSLKeyShare::Create() has already been through tls1_check_group_id(),
    which accepts only what is in supported_group_list. Should any path reach
    Create() with one of these anyway, it now returns nullptr -- which all
    five of its call sites already check, failing the handshake rather than
    dereferencing it.
  - ML-DSA appears in kSignSignatureAlgorithms, but the server signs with a
    P-256 ECDSA certificate and the client's kVerifySignatureAlgorithms does
    not list ML-DSA at all, so neither side can produce or accept one.

They are linked in anyway, because BoringSSL reaches every algorithm it
implements from static tables that --gc-sections cannot prune. Upstream is
aware of the shape of this -- see the comment above the array in
ssl_privkey.cc: "Ideally the set of reachable algorithms would flow from
|SSL_CTX| for dead code elimination, but for now we just specify every
algorithm that might be reachable from libssl."

Five tables reference them, and all five have to go for the linker to drop
the code:

  crypto/evp/internal.h        default set for EVP_parse_{public,private}_key
  crypto/evp/evp_ctx.cc        key-type -> algorithm lookup
  ssl/ssl_key_share.cc         TLS key-share factory
  ssl/ssl_privkey.cc           SPKI parsing inside libssl
  pki/verify_signed_data.cc    SPKI parsing for certificate verification

This narrows default sets; it deletes no implementation. Every one of these
algorithms remains compiled and directly callable through its public API.
Entries that are merely enum constants -- the ML-DSA rows of
kSignatureAlgorithms, the ssl_is_key_type_supported cases, the
SignatureAlgorithm::kMldsa* cases in verify_signed_data.cc -- are deliberately
left alone: they pull in no code, so removing them would be behaviour change
without benefit.

Verified against BoringSSL's own suite -- crypto_test, ssl_test, pki_test and
decrepit_test -- built and run on this project's own musl, static-pie,
compiler-rt toolchain. 3599 cases execute (crypto_test 1798, ssl_test 468,
pki_test 1321, decrepit_test 12; --gtest_list_tests reports a slightly higher
number because some are filtered at run time). All 3599 pass unpatched.

The expected result with the patch is not "all green". These eleven fail, and
they are the acceptance criterion: every one of them exercises precisely the
defaults this patch narrows, and a failure outside this list means the patch
has done something it should not.

  crypto_test  EVPTest.MLDSATestVectors
               EVPTest.MLKEMTestVectors
               EVPExtraTest.MLDSAKeyGen
               EVPExtraTest.NewRawKey
               X509Test.TestMlDsa65
               X509Test.TestMlDsa65BadParameters
  ssl_test     SSLTest.ServerSupportedGroupsHint
  pki_test     VerifySignedDataTest.Mldsa44
               VerifySignedDataTest.Mldsa65
               VerifySignedDataTest.Mldsa87
               VerifyTest.MldsaAlgorithms
  decrepit_test  (none -- all 12 pass)

To re-run after a version bump:

  bazel test @boringssl//:crypto_test @boringssl//:ssl_test \
             @boringssl//:pki_test @boringssl//:decrepit_test \
             --platforms=//build/toolchain:linux_x64 -c opt --dynamic_mode=off

--dynamic_mode=off is required: these tests pull in gtest, which Bazel would
otherwise build as a shared library, and this toolchain links -static-pie.

